                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Sig.NULL is a puzzle game that looks simple at first but gets much more complicated once I start understanding how everything works. I am controlling small mono drones inside a strange computer system and my main job is to move blocks into their correct positions. At first it feels like a fairly normal Sokoban style puzzle, but the game slowly starts introducing different drone abilities and hidden rules that completely change how I approach each level. I like that the game does not explain everything immediately because figuring out how the world works is a big part of the experience.\n\nThe best thing about Sig.NULL for me is the way the puzzles keep adding new ideas. There are more than 100 levels and up to eight different types of drones, with abilities that can involve things like exploding, swapping, infecting blocks and controlling time. Some drones can also affect other objects in ways that I do not initially expect. This means I am not simply pushing one box around a room and looking for an obvious route. I have to understand how several things interact and sometimes completely rethink what I thought a particular object was supposed to do.\n\nI also really like the non linear level structure. I am normally given several levels to choose from, so if I get stuck on one puzzle I can leave it alone and try another. That is a much better approach for a difficult puzzle game because I do not feel like one particularly annoying level is completely blocking my progress. The game also gradually introduces its mechanics, so even though the later puzzles can become very difficult, I am given time to experiment with the rules first. When I finally understand a solution that had been confusing me, it feels properly earned.\n\nThe atmosphere is another thing that works surprisingly well. The whole game looks like I am moving through the inside of some strange computer system, with dark colours, grids and mechanical sounds giving it a cold cybernetic feeling. There is not a traditional story constantly interrupting the puzzles, but there is a mysterious sense that I am exploring a world with its own rules and trying to reach the system core. I like that the presentation stays focused on the puzzles rather than trying to turn a simple block pushing game into something it does not need to be.\n\nThe biggest weakness for me is the difficulty curve. Sig.NULL can be extremely unclear when it introduces a new mechanic, and there are times when I am not sure whether I am supposed to solve a puzzle or discover another rule first. I enjoy working things out for myself, but the game can sometimes leave me with too little information to understand what I am even experimenting with. Some puzzles are satisfying because I eventually see the solution, while others can feel like I am randomly testing possibilities until something finally makes sense. That makes the experience more demanding than a normal Sokoban game.\n\nI also think the dark presentation can make some details harder to read than they should be. It fits the computer world perfectly, but objects and colours can sometimes blend together, especially when I am trying to understand several moving pieces at once. The game is also very focused on one type of puzzle, so there is not much here for me if I am looking for action, exploration or a more traditional adventure. I have to enjoy sitting down and thinking carefully because that is almost the entire point of the game.\n\nOverall, Sig.NULL is a clever and unusually challenging puzzle game that I think gets stronger the more I understand it. The large number of levels, different drone abilities, hidden rules and non linear progression give me plenty to think about, while the strange computer atmosphere gives the whole thing its own identity. The steep difficulty and sometimes limited explanation can definitely frustrate me, but I think that is also part of what makes solving the harder puzzles feel rewarding. It is not a game I would recommend when I want something relaxing or effortless, but when I want something that actually makes me stop and think, Sig.NULL does a very good job.\n\nRating: 7.4/10\n",
